# Debug session best practices

## Use wait_for_pause instead of polling

After any execution control call (`start_debug_session`, `resume_execution`, `step_over`, `step_into`, `step_out`, `run_to_line`), call `wait_for_pause` with an appropriate `timeout` (seconds) to block until execution stops. This returns the full session status (location, variables, stack) and eliminates manual polling loops.

## Always clean up sessions

Call `stop_debug_session` when done debugging. Abandoned sessions keep the target process running and consume IDE resources.

## Discover before starting

Call `list_run_configurations` before `start_debug_session` to get the exact `configuration_name`. Typos in the name will fail silently.

## Breakpoint file paths must be absolute

`set_breakpoint` and `run_to_line` require `file_path` as an **absolute** path (unlike most Index/GeneralIDE tools which take relative paths).

## Breakpoint paths must be IntelliJ-debuggable

Use files under the **project** (or otherwise on SDK/source roots the IDE already attaches). Paths to extracted JDK sources in `/tmp` (or other copies outside the configured SDK) typically **fail** to verify. For line breakpoints, prefer project `src/...`. For **method** breakpoints on JDK classes, use IntelliJ’s UI — the Debugger MCP only supports **line** breakpoints in accepted files.

## Expression evaluation language limits

`evaluate_expression` and `set_variable` have full support in Java, Kotlin, Python, and JavaScript. For Rust, C++, and Go (LLDB/GDB), method calls may not work — variable inspection is reliable but complex expressions are not.
